# Landing a change

The hooks that enforce all of this print the next command when they fire — read their output
rather than working around them.

## Branches

| Branch             | Role                                            | Direct commits |
| ------------------ | ----------------------------------------------- | -------------- |
| `main`             | Release. Only ever merged into, from `develop`. | No             |
| `develop`          | Integration, and the default place to work.     | Yes            |
| `<type>/<summary>` | Short-lived, opened from `develop`.             | Yes            |

A small, self-contained change can go straight onto `develop`. Anything that wants review, spans
several commits, or might need reverting gets a branch and a pull request into `develop`.

```bash
git switch develop
git switch -c feat/price-offer-toggle
```

The type is the Conventional Commits set: `feat` `fix` `docs` `refactor` `perf` `test` `build`
`ci` `chore` `revert`. The summary is lower-case kebab-case.

## Commit

```
<type>(<scope>): <subject, imperative, lower case, no period, 12–72 chars>

<body: why this was needed, wrapped at 100 columns>
```

Scope is optional but must come from `app` `pages` `widgets` `entities` `features` `shared` `seed`
`i18n` `styles` `ci` `deps` `ai` `repo`. Omit it rather than inventing one.

The body explains why; the diff already shows what. Placeholder subjects — `update`, `changes`,
`wip`, `apply feedback` — are rejected by commitlint. Keep one concern per commit.

Never write about the tooling that produced a change. A commit message is read by someone
debugging in six months, not by someone watching you work.

## Before pushing

`pnpm verify` is exactly what CI runs. Green locally means green on the pull request.

## Pull request

Base is `develop`. The title is a Conventional Commits line describing the whole branch, because
squash merge turns it into a single commit. Fill `.github/PULL_REQUEST_TEMPLATE.md`: why, what
changed as behaviour, how it was verified, and anything a reviewer would otherwise have to infer.

## Release (`develop` → `main`)

Merging into `main` is a release. Full contract in [CONTRIBUTING.md](../../CONTRIBUTING.md#릴리스).

1. On `develop`, bump `package.json` `version` (SemVer from Conventional Commits since the last
   `v*` tag) and add a `CHANGELOG.md` section.
2. Commit `chore(repo): release vX.Y.Z`, push `develop`, open a PR with base `main`.
3. Merge with a **merge commit** — never squash or rebase a release PR.
4. The `release` workflow on `main` creates the `vX.Y.Z` tag and GitHub Release from
   `package.json`. Do not tag on `develop`.

## Reviewing

Use the rubric in `.cursor/commands/review.md`. The order matters: design-system violations come
first because they fail silently — a non-SEED utility emits no CSS, so the page renders unstyled
and nothing errors.

## Never

- `--no-verify`, or `eslint-disable` on a `seed-lockin/*` rule
- committing to `main`, or force-pushing `main` or `develop`
- amending a commit that a hook rejected — fix the cause and make a new one
- hand-editing `src/app/routeTree.gen.ts`, `.seed/tokens.json`, or the generated token rule
- squash-merging a release PR, or creating `v*` tags on `develop`
